From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wangzlei <66336933+wangzlei@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 16:32:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] go-request-host: read net/http.Request.Host in the Go
 HTTP probes

Upstream's Go uprobes read only Request.URL.Host. Two consequences:
- client: a Go client that dials an IP and sets req.Host = "audit.internal:8000"
  (Go then sends req.Host as the Host header) gets server.address = the IP;
- server: a server-side Request.URL comes from the request-target and has no
  host, so Go server spans have no host and patch 04's Go ForwardedHost (and
  with it peer.service.name on HTTP server RED behind an ALB) is always empty.

This patch adds the net/http.Request.Host offset (DWARF, and the prefetched
offsets.json / tracker input for stripped binaries: 128 since Go 1.17) and
reads it in the BPF probes:
- roundTrip: after URL.Host, Request.Host overrides it when non-empty, which is
  exactly what Go's transport puts on the wire;
- ServeHTTP: Request.Host (Host header for HTTP/1, :authority for HTTP/2) is
  stored in server_http_func_invocation_t.host (+100 B per map entry) and copied
  into the server trace's host, which used to be forced empty. The Go server
  span then carries the Host like the kprobe path already does.

Degrades to upstream behaviour: the new offset table slot is initialised to the
missing sentinel (~0) and the BPF helper skips the read when the offset is
unknown or the string is empty. No new map, no stack growth (the server copy
lives in the existing per-CPU scratch / LRU map value; the client reuses its
host buffer).

Why upstream does not do it: OBI took the host from URL.Host only, which is
correct for most clients and was never wired for servers.

Turn it off: not configurable (it only fills a host that was empty or replaces
it by the value Go sends on the wire); drop this patch to get upstream back.

Adot-Obi-Patch: 06-go-request-host
